      "body": "Some MI355X data in support of keeping `aiter` as FastH3's transformer default on ROCm. Setup: `lmsysorg/sglang-rocm:v0.5.21-rocm10-mi35x-20261014`, 1× MI355X.\n\n- **The kernel itself works on gfx950.** The in-tree VSA-H3 Triton forward passes `test_vsa_h3_attention.py` (12 passed).\n- **It is slower than dense aiter at 10% block density.**\n\n  | Tokens (24 heads, head 128) | VSA-H3 Triton | Dense aiter |\n  | --- | ---: | ---: |\n  | 64k | 69.4 ms | 42.5 ms |\n  | 32k | 17.6 ms | 10.6 ms |\n\n  The VSA-H3 numbers use `num_stages=2, waves_per_eu=2, matrix_instr_nonkdim=16`. The pinned Hopper/Blackwell launch (`num_warps=4, num_stages=5`) is slower still, at 91.3 ms for 64k.\n- **End to end it would need more work.** Routing `video_sparse_attn_h3` on ROCm stops in `flash_attention_v3.flash_attn_varlen_func` with `No module named 'flash_attn'`.\n- **The aiter default works.** FastH3 V2 t2va at 1344×768, 5 s, 8 steps takes 34.8 s on one MI355X.\n\nSo the CUDA-only VSA-H3 default looks like the right call for ROCm today.\n",
      "created_at": "2026-10-15T17:12:29Z",
      "html_url": "https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/pull/44421#issuecomment-6197884956",
      "id": 6197884956,
      "updated_at": "2026-10-15T17:12:29Z",
      "user": {
        "login": "stayinalive181"
      }
    },
    {
      "body": "Thanks for the data. To be clear on scope, this PR is test-only and doesn't change backend selection: FastH3 still defaults to VSA-H3 only on CUDA, and ROCm keeps its `aiter` default. The new `test_fasth3_keeps_the_rocm_default_backend` case pins that, so routing ROCm to VSA-H3 later would mean updating that test on purpose. @stayinalive181 \r\n",
